# SEMANTICS TEST (auto-accept-prior-states head gap on the to_client direction,
# failing keyword, matching accept:flow at the < rule's own hook -- the to_client
# variant of ruletype-firewall-216).
#
# A head non-LTE accept:flow rule at server_hello (hook 1) whose keyword matches, followed
# by a <server_hello (auto-accept-prior-states) rule at the same hook whose version
# keyword does NOT match.
#
# The to_client tx is presented directly at progress 1 (the server hello is a single
# record -- no fragmented partial phase), and detect_progress_orig is 0
# (server_in_progress). So there is a head-of-list gap: the head rule's hook (1) is
# above the original progress (0). The ONLY rule that could authorize the gap state
# (hook 0) is the < rule (via its prior-states coverage); on the first to_client
# detection pass the < rule is a pending candidate (its tls.version:1.3 keyword is
# only final at its own hook state), so its auto-accept-prior-states covers the gap
# state at the moment the head-gap decision is made.
#
# Auto-accept-prior-states semantics: the prior to_client state (hook 0) is
# auto-accepted while the < rule is pending and is NOT revoked by the < rule's keyword
# no-match at the rule's own hook state -- the keyword only decides the final action at
# that own hook state (server_hello), where the head rule's matching accept:flow
# accepts the flow anyway.
#
# Expected: 0 to_client "firewall default app policy" drops -- the gap state is
# accepted by the pending phase and the flow is accepted at server_hello. (A reading
# that retroactively revokes the prior-state auto-accept when the < rule's keyword fails
# at its own hook would drop the gap state and FAIL here.)
#
# to_server is fully authorized by bare accept:hook rules at every TLS client state
# (sids 701-705) so the to_server side never drops and does not mask the to_client
# adjudication.
#
# Pcap: ../../tls/tls-client-hello-frag-01 (server hello is TLSv1.2).

# allow session setup
accept:hook tcp:all $HOME_NET any <> $EXTERNAL_NET any (flow:not_established; sid:1021;)
accept:hook tcp:all $HOME_NET any <> $EXTERNAL_NET any (flow:established; sid:1022;)

# to_server shield: authorize every to_server TLS state with bare accept:hook rules so
# the to_server side never drops and the to_client adjudication is not masked by an
# early flow drop
accept:hook tls:client_in_progress $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (sid:701;)
accept:hook tls:client_hello_done $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (sid:702;)
accept:hook tls:client_cert_done $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (sid:703;)
accept:hook tls:client_handshake_done $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (sid:704;)
accept:hook tls:client_finished $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (sid:705;)

# Head to_client rule (non-LTE) at server_hello (hook 1): version 1.2 matches the pcap
# (TLSv1.2). It authorizes the server_hello state (and later), but NOT hook 0
# (server_in_progress) -- that state is only covered by the < rule below.
accept:flow tls:server_hello $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any (tls.version:1.2; sid:621;)

# Auto-accept-prior-states at server_hello (hook 1) -> the only rule that could
# authorize hook 0. Its version keyword (1.3) does NOT match the pcap (TLSv1.2), so it
# authorizes nothing and covers no state.
accept:flow tls:<server_hello $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any (tls.version:1.3; sid:622;)
